    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1530497610245-94d3c16cda28?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "content": "> **Research Takeaway:** MOTS-c (Mitochondrial Open Reading Frame of the 12S rRNA-c) is a 16-amino acid peptide encoded within the mitochondrial genome. It functions as an endocrine metabolic regulator by activating AMPK, stimulating GLUT4 glucose translocation, and restoring metabolic flexibility.\n\n## Executive Overview: The Mitochondrial Genome as a Signaling Hub\n\nHistorically, mitochondria were viewed solely as the energetic powerhouses of eukaryotic cells. In 2015, researchers discovered that the mitochondrial 12S ribosomal RNA gene encodes bioactive signaling peptides termed **Mitochondrial-Derived Peptides (MDPs)**.\n\nThe most prominent MDP is **MOTS-c**, a 16-amino acid sequence that translocates to the nucleus under cellular stress to regulate nuclear gene expression and systemic metabolism.\n\n---\n\n## Chemical Profile & Sequence\n\n* **Sequence:** Met-Arg-Trp-Gln-Glu-Met-Gly-Tyr-Ile-Phe-Tyr-Pro-Arg-Lys-Leu-Arg (MRWQEMGYIFYPRKLR)\n* **Molecular Formula:** C101H152N28O22S2\n* **Molecular Weight:** 2174.62 g/mol\n* **Genomic Origin:** Mitochondrial DNA (12S rRNA locus)\n\n![Peptra Bio MOTS-c Research Product](/images/products/mots-c.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Key Intracellular Signaling Pathways\n\n### 1. AMPK Activation & AICAR Accumulation\nMOTS-c inhibits the folate-methionine cycle, resulting in the accumulation of **AICAR** (5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide ribonucleotide). Elevated AICAR levels directly phosphorylate and activate **AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK)**, the master metabolic switch of the cell.\n\n### 2. Muscle Glucose Uptake & GLUT4 Translocation\nIn skeletal muscle cell assays, MOTS-c stimulates the translocation of **GLUT4** glucose transporters to the cell membrane independently of insulin, enhancing glucose uptake and fatty acid oxidation.\n\n### 3. Nuclear Translocation & Stress Adaptation\nDuring metabolic stress (such as nutrient deprivation or intense oxidative stress), MOTS-c translocates into the cell nucleus, binding directly to antioxidant-response elements to promote cellular survival.\n\n---\n\n## Preclinical Research Findings\n\n| Experimental Model | Observed Biological Outcomes | Key Biomarkers |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Diet-Induced Obesity (Rodents)** | Prevention of insulin resistance, enhanced lipid clearance | Lower fasting insulin, improved HOMA-IR |\n| **Skeletal Muscle Performance** | Increased running capacity, elevated mitochondrial biogenesis | PGC-1α upregulation, higher citrate synthase |\n| **Aging & Metabolic Flexibility** | Maintenance of glucose clearance in aged mice | Normalized glucose tolerance curves |\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)\n\n### How was MOTS-c discovered?\nMOTS-c was identified in 2015 by researchers at the University of Southern California (Lee et al.) who discovered short open reading frames (sORFs) within the mitochondrial 12S rRNA gene.\n\n### Why is MOTS-c called an \"exercise mimetic\"?\nIn preclinical studies, MOTS-c activates the same AMPK and PGC-1alpha metabolic cascades triggered by endurance exercise, improving physical capacity and skeletal muscle insulin sensitivity in mice.\n\n### How does MOTS-c stimulate glucose uptake without insulin?\nMOTS-c activates the AMPK signaling pathway, which causes GLUT4 glucose transporter vesicles to fuse with the muscle cell membrane through an insulin-independent mechanism.\n\n### What are the stability requirements for MOTS-c?\nMOTS-c contains methionine residues sensitive to oxidation. Lyophilized powder should be stored at -20°C in an airtight container. Reconstituted solutions should be stored at 2°C–8°C and protected from oxygen and light.\n\n### Is MOTS-c approved for treating diabetes or metabolic disease?\nNo. MOTS-c is an investigational compound used exclusively in laboratory and animal research.\n\n---\n\n## Research Use Only Disclaimer\n\nThis article is prepared exclusively for scientific education and academic reference. Peptra Bio supplies peptides strictly for laboratory in vitro investigation.\n\n---\n\n## Key References\n\n1. Lee C, et al. *The mitochondrial-derived peptide MOTS-c promotes metabolic homeostasis and reduces obesity and insulin resistance.* Cell Metab. 2015;21(3):443-454.\n2. Reynolds JC, et al. *MOTS-c is an exercise-induced mitochondrial-encoded regulator of walking capacity and muscle homeostasis.* Nat Commun. 2021;12(1):470.\n3. Kim KH, et al. *Mitochondrial-derived peptides in aging and metabolic disease.* Exp Mol Med. 2017;49(4):e317."
