    "id": "blog-how-aod-9604-works-lipolytic-domain",
    "slug": "how-aod-9604-works-lipolytic-domain",
    "title": "How AOD-9604 Works: Lipolytic C-Terminal Domain of Human Growth Hormone",
    "excerpt": "Understand the biochemical mechanisms of the modified hGH fragment 177-191 (AOD-9604) in stimulating adipose beta-3 lipolysis without IGF-1 modulation.",
    "category": "Compound Spotlights",
    "tags": [
      "AOD-9604",
      "hGH Fragment",
      "Lipolysis",
      "Adipose Biology",
      "Metabolic Research"
    ],
    "readTime": "8 min read",
    "author": "Dr. Marcus Vance, Ph.D.",
    "publishedAt": "2025-02-15",
    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1579154204601-01588f351e67?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "content": "> **Research Takeaway:** AOD-9604 is a synthetic 16-amino acid peptide derived from the C-terminal lipolytic domain of human Growth Hormone (hGH 177-191). By isolating the fat-metabolizing segment from the somatogenic core, it stimulates lipolysis without altering IGF-1 or blood glucose levels.\n\n## Executive Overview: Isolating the Lipolytic Domain of Somatotropin\n\nFull-length Human Growth Hormone (hGH) is a 191-amino acid polypeptide that exhibits both anabolic (growth-promoting) and lipolytic (fat-reducing) actions. However, full hGH also induces hyperinsulinemia and elevates systemic IGF-1.\n\nResearchers at Monash University engineered **AOD-9604** by isolating the functional C-terminal fragment (residues 177–191) and adding an N-terminal tyrosine residue with a disulfide cyclic bridge to preserve the bioactive conformation.\n\n---\n\n## Molecular Architecture & Chemical Profile\n\n* **Sequence:** Tyr-Leu-Arg-Ile-Val-Gln-Cys-Arg-Ser-Val-Glu-Gly-Ser-Cys-Gly-Phe (with an internal Cys7–Cys14 disulfide bridge)\n* **Molecular Weight:** 1815.1 g/mol\n* **Active Region:** Mimics the lipolytic C-terminus of hGH while lacking the N-terminal somatogenic domain responsible for IGF-1 generation.\n\n![Peptra Bio AOD-9604 Research Vial](/images/products/aod-9604.jpg)\n\n---\n\n## Dual Mechanism of Action on Adipose Tissue\n\n### 1. Stimulation of Lipolysis\nAOD-9604 upregulates **Beta-3 Adrenergic Receptor (β3-AR)** signaling on adipocytes, stimulating hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) to break down stored triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol.\n\n### 2. Inhibition of Lipogenesis\nIn in vitro fat cell assays, AOD-9604 inhibits the enzymes responsible for converting circulating glucose into fat stores (acetyl-CoA carboxylase and glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase).\n\n---\n\n## Comparison: AOD-9604 vs Full-Length hGH (191 AA)\n\n| Biological Parameter | AOD-9604 (16 AA Fragment) | Full-Length hGH (191 AA) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Lipolytic Activity** | High (Targeted adipose breakdown) | High |\n| **IGF-1 Generation** | None (0% increase) | High (Stimulates hepatic IGF-1) |\n| **Insulin Resistance Risk** | None (Does not impair glucose uptake) | High with repeated exposure |\n| **Mitogenic Growth Effects** | None | High (Cell proliferation across tissues)|\n| **Molecular Stability** | Stabilized by internal disulfide bond | Susceptible to rapid proteolysis |\n\n---\n\n## Preclinical Cartilage & Chondrocyte Research\n\nIn addition to adipose studies, recent in vitro and animal models have investigated AOD-9604 in **cartilage regeneration**:\n* When co-administered with hyaluronic acid in rabbit osteoarthritis models, AOD-9604 stimulated chondrocyte proliferation and proteoglycan deposition in articular cartilage defects.\n\n---\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)\n\n### Why doesn't AOD-9604 increase IGF-1 or blood sugar?\nThe IGF-1 stimulating and diabetogenic domains of growth hormone reside in the N-terminal and central regions of the 191-amino acid protein. AOD-9604 contains only the C-terminal 177-191 fragment, completely isolating lipolytic signaling.\n\n### What is the role of the disulfide bridge in AOD-9604?\nThe disulfide bridge between Cysteine-7 and Cysteine-14 maintains the cyclic loop conformation necessary for binding to adipocyte target receptors and protects the peptide from immediate enzymatic degradation.\n\n### How is AOD-9604 reconstituted in the lab?\nAOD-9604 dissolves well in bacteriostatic water. For highly concentrated solutions, a slightly acidified sterile diluent may assist in rapid dissolution.\n\n### What are the storage requirements?\nStore dry lyophilized vials at -20°C. Once reconstituted, refrigerate at 2°C–8°C and utilize within 28 days.\n\n### Is AOD-9604 approved for clinical human weight loss?\nNo. AOD-9604 is an investigational peptide strictly manufactured for laboratory and preclinical animal research.\n\n---\n\n## Research Use Only Disclaimer\n\nThis technical document is prepared solely for scientific education and laboratory research reference. Peptra Bio supplies compounds strictly for in vitro laboratory experimentation.\n\n---\n\n## Key References\n\n1. Ng FM, et al. *Metabolic actions of a synthetic C-terminal fragment of human growth hormone (AOD9604).* J Mol Endocrinol. 2000;25(3):287-298.\n2. Heffernan M, et al. *The effects of human GH and its lipolytic fragment (AOD9604) on lipid metabolism in obese mice.* Endocrinology. 2001;142(12):5182-5189.\n3. Kwon DR, et al. *Effect of intra-articular injection of AOD9604 with hyaluronic acid in a rabbit model of osteoarthritis.* Ann Clin Lab Sci. 2015;45(4):426-432."
